  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "The lowest common divisor (LCD) of a sequence of kkk integers (x1,x2,…,xk)(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_k)(x1​,x2​,…,xk​) is the **smallest** integer ddd such that:\n\n\nd&gt;1d\\gt 1d&gt;1, and\nddd is a **divisor** of xix_ixi​ for all 1≤i≤k1\\le i \\le k1≤i≤k.\nIf no such integer ddd exists, the LCD of the sequence is defined to be 111.\n\nFor example,\n\n\nThe LCD of (6,15,6,21)(6, 15, 6, 21)(6,15,6,21) is 333.\nThe LCD of (9,9)(9, 9)(9,9) is 333.\nThe LCD of (10,20)(10, 20)(10,20) is 222.\nThe LCD of (6,10,15)(6, 10, 15)(6,10,15) is 111.\nYou're given an array AAA containing NNN integers, and an integer KKK.\n\nCount the number of non-empty subsequences of AAA whose LCD equals KKK.\n\nSince this number can be large, print it modulo 109+710^9 + 7109+7.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and KKK — the length of the array and the required LCD.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​ — the elements of array AAA.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line, the number of non-empty subsequences of AAA whose LCD is KKK, modulo 109+710^9 + 7109+7.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1001 \\leq T \\leq 1001≤T≤100",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 2\\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "1≤Ai≤1091 \\leq A_i \\leq 10^91≤Ai​≤109",
      "2≤K≤602 \\leq K \\leq 602≤K≤60",
      "The sum of NNN across all tests won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3 2\n5 4 6\n5 3\n1 6 12 6 9\n5 4\n1 6 16 6 9",
        "output": "3\n8\n0",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: There are 333 subsequences with LCD 222. These are [4],[6],[4], [6],[4],[6], and [4,6][4,6][4,6].\nTest case 222: There are 888 subsequences with LCD 333. These are [9],[6,9],[12,9],[6,9],[6,12,9],[6,6,9],[12,6,9],[9], [6, 9], [12, 9], [6, 9], [6, 12, 9], [6, 6, 9], [12, 6, 9],[9],[6,9],[12,9],[6,9],[6,12,9],[6,6,9],[12,6,9], and [6,12,6,9][6, 12, 6, 9][6,12,6,9]."
      }
    ]
  },
